item.text:
Searches covered the EC appointment statute and implementation, 2022 local-election violence and contestability, EC independence, DSA implementation, group participation, caretaker-government policy, shutdown attribution, and year-on-year democracy measures. The strongest additions are the appointment-law implementation trail, the EC’s cancellation of the Gaibandha-5 vote, CGS case-level DSA findings, and two independently grounded baseline comparisons. These sources also supply contrary evidence: opposition rallies occurred, non-AL candidates won local races, women’s local representation improved, and aggregate democracy measures were stable or marginally higher.

Important unresolved gaps remain. I found no publicly accessible original BTRC order or carrier telemetry; the reported Rajshahi directive could not be opened reliably and is therefore omitted. I found the governing party’s direct 2022 caretaker position, but not a target-year statement personally delivered by Hasina. Nor did I find evidence that Hasina personally accepted the Gaibandha cancellation; it demonstrates EC action under her government, not her own restraint. Official nationwide EC tables consolidating 2022 turnout, complaints, uncontested seats, and violence were not recoverable. Group-specific evidence remains strongest for women and weakest for enforceable remedies affecting religious, ethnic, and LGBT+ minorities.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Govt publishes gazette on CEC, ECs appointment law","publisher":"Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha","publication_date":"2022-01-30","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news/41527","claim":"The gazetted 2022 appointment law required the president to constitute a six-member search committee, including two judges nominated by the chief justice, the auditor general, the Public Service Commission chair, and two presidentially nominated citizens, and required two recommendations per vacancy within 15 working days. This created Bangladesh's first statutory EC-selection framework but retained substantial appointment influence in state officeholders and presidential nominees.","locator":"Paragraphs under headline, especially lines 45-75: enactment and assent at 45-52; committee composition at 53-60; deadline and voting rules at 61-70; qualifications at 72-75","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-001","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_ec_appointment_act_structure","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2"],"source_type":"official_state_news_report_of_gazette","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous state-news reproduction of the gazetted statute's operative provisions; the underlying gazette is identified, although the official legal-database page was not retrievable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly records enactment and operative provisions in January 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Government and parliamentary conduct under Hasina's premiership; the source does not establish her personal drafting or direction.","contrary_evidence":"The statute imposed qualifications, a deadline, plural committee membership, and chief-justice nomination of two judicial members, all of which are formal safeguards.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_ec_appointment_act_structure","lenses":["4B.2"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"EC search committee reveals 322 names","publisher":"Dhaka Tribune","publication_date":"2022-02-14","url":"https://www.dhakatribune.com/bangladesh/election/287233/ec-search-committee-reveals-322-names","claim":"The statutory search committee disclosed 322 proposed EC candidates and solicited nominations from parties, professional bodies, and individuals, but did not identify which entity nominated each person; former bureaucrats, judges, police, military officers, and incumbent or former senior state officials dominated the list, while BNP declined to participate because it lacked confidence in the process.","locator":"Lines 135-151 and 180-188: disclosure and missing nominator identities at 145-148; composition at 148-151; committee membership and nomination participation at 180-188","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-002","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_ec_search_committee_implementation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2"],"source_type":"independent_news_reporting","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous detailed reporting tied to the committee's published list and gazette, with named participants and explicit limits on disclosure.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct February 2022 implementation evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional process conducted under Hasina's government; no evidence of her personal selection of nominees.","contrary_evidence":"Publishing all 322 names was more transparent than prior search processes, and the committee extended the deadline and invited BNP participation.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_ec_search_committee_implementation","lenses":["4B.2"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Ex-defence secretary Habibul Awal appointed CEC","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-02-26","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/elections/news/ex-defence-secretary-habibul-awal-appointed-cec-2971076","claim":"The president appointed former defence secretary Kazi Habibul Awal as CEC and four commissioners after the search committee consulted parties and experts and published 322 proposed names; however, the committee withheld its final shortlist of ten, and BNP and other opposition parties did not nominate candidates because they distrusted the process.","locator":"Lines 159-173: appointments at 159-160; consultation and publication at 163-168; withheld shortlist and opposition nonparticipation at 168-173","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-003","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_ec_commission_appointments","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.2"],"source_type":"independent_news_reporting_of_official_appointment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting identifies the gazette appointments and distinguishes completed consultation from the undisclosed final shortlist.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct February 2022 appointment evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Presidential appointment within the constitutional system operating under Hasina's government; personal intervention by Hasina is not shown.","contrary_evidence":"The process included public nominations, expert consultations, and broader disclosure than the 2012 and 2017 processes.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_ec_commission_appointments","lenses":["4B.2"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Report: 2,249 cases registered under DSA in 2022","publisher":"Dhaka Tribune","publication_date":"2022-12-31","url":"https://www.dhakatribune.com/bangladesh/301705/report-2-249-cases-registered-under-dsa-in-2022","claim":"Ain o Salish Kendra's year-end monitoring identified 479 incidents of political conflict and violence during local elections at various stages in 2022, providing nationwide contextual evidence that local electoral competition remained substantially violent.","locator":"Headline and lines 142-162, especially summary at 144 and ASK attribution at 152-161","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-004","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_local_election_political_violence_ask","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.1"],"source_type":"independent_human_rights_monitoring_reported_by_press","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"The claim is attributed to a named national rights monitor's annual report, but the accessible article does not expose incident-level rows or clarify whether all 479 incidents were uniquely election-related rather than broader political conflict.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Explicit calendar-year 2022 aggregate.","ruler_attribution":"National outcome under Hasina's government; shared among parties, supporters, election administrators, and security forces unless individual incidents establish narrower responsibility.","contrary_evidence":"The aggregate does not establish that government or AL actors initiated every incident.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_local_election_political_violence_ask","lenses":["4B.1"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"EC cancels Gaibandha-5 by-election","publisher":"Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news/87584","claim":"After monitoring polling through CCTV, the Election Commission stopped voting at 51 centers and cancelled the entire Gaibandha-5 by-election because one group or candidate was influencing polling and the vote was not impartial or fair. The ruling Awami League had a candidate in the contest.","locator":"Lines 41-51, especially statutory basis at 44, CCTV findings and cancellation rationale at 47-49, and candidate list at 51","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-005","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_gaibandha_ec_cancellation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.1","4B.4"],"source_type":"official_election_decision_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official account quotes the CEC, identifies the statutory basis, the number of affected centers, the monitoring method, and the decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct October 2022 institutional action.","ruler_attribution":"Evidence of an EC constraint operating during Hasina's premiership, not proof that Hasina personally welcomed, ordered, or accepted the cancellation.","contrary_evidence":"The cancellation is significant contrary evidence against a claim that the EC invariably certified a ruling-party electoral outcome.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_gaibandha_ec_cancellation","lenses":["4B.1","4B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"EC gets detailed report on Gaibandha by-poll irregularities","publisher":"Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news/94090","claim":"The EC received a detailed investigation report following its cancellation of the Gaibandha-5 by-election, said it would disclose detailed information and punish persons found involved, and reported that field administration subsequently responded to instructions in other elections. This documents follow-up rather than cancellation alone, though it does not prove that promised sanctions were imposed.","locator":"Lines 44-59: receipt and intended disclosure at 44-50; instructions and claimed subsequent administrative compliance at 52-59","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-006","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_gaibandha_ec_followup","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.4"],"source_type":"official_election_followup_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Directly quotes an election commissioner and confirms receipt of an investigation report, but promised disclosure and punishment are prospective and the article does not contain the report or completed remedies.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct November 2022 follow-up.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional EC conduct under Hasina's government; no personal Hasina acceptance or corrective instruction is established.","contrary_evidence":"The EC pursued an inquiry and issued instructions rather than simply allowing the contested result to stand.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2022_gaibandha_ec_followup","lenses":["4B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Cases Under DSA: Almost all accused kept hanging","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2023-01-15","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/cases-under-dsa-almost-all-accused-kept-hanging-3221031","claim":"CGS's case-level dataset covering October 2018-August 2022 found only 52 of 2,889 identified accused persons had obtained a conviction, acquittal, or dismissal, three quarters of recorded cases remained under police investigation, and at least 725 pre-2022 cases had exceeded the DSA's investigation deadline. This implementation evidence weighs against official claims that the Act functioned primarily as an ordinary, efficiently adjudicated cybercrime law.","locator":"Lines 159-184: dataset scope and disposition at 159-172; overdue investigations and statutory 60/75-day limit at 174-178; arrest and coverage limitations at 179-184","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-007","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_dsa_case_disposition_through_august_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.6"],"source_type":"independent_case_dataset_reported_by_press","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The report specifies dataset dates, denominators, outcomes, statutory deadlines, and collection limitations; publication was later but specifically establishes facts through August 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Dataset extends through August 2022 and specifically measures implementation during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Implementation by police, prosecutors, tribunals, and complainants under Hasina's government; responsibility for the systemic policy environment is stronger than attribution of each case to Hasina personally.","contrary_evidence":"Some cases did reach conviction, acquittal, dismissal, or withdrawal, and CGS acknowledges that its media- and tribunal-derived data are incomplete.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_dsa_case_disposition_through_august_2022","lenses":["4B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Cases Under DSA: Almost all accused kept hanging","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2023-01-15","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/cases-under-dsa-almost-all-accused-kept-hanging-3221031","claim":"CGS identified politicians and journalists as the two largest known occupational groups among DSA accused persons; ruling-party affiliates were the largest group prosecuting journalists, with 140 recorded cases alleging defamation of Sheikh Hasina and 64 alleging defamation of ministers. These patterns independently connect implementation to protection of political incumbents rather than cybercrime alone, while not showing Hasina personally initiated the complaints.","locator":"Lines 186-200, especially occupational groups at 186-189, defamation complainant pattern at 189-195, AL and Chhatra League filing totals at 195-197, and CGS conclusion at 198-200","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-008","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_dsa_political_complainant_pattern_through_august_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.6"],"source_type":"independent_case_dataset_reported_by_press","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The underlying study separates accused occupations, complainant affiliations, protected political figures, and case totals, permitting a direct test of official justification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Dataset includes implementation through August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The cases protected Hasina or ministers and were often filed by AL affiliates, but the source does not establish that Hasina directed or approved individual complaints.","contrary_evidence":"The dataset does not claim every DSA prosecution was political, and incomplete official disclosure prevents a census of all cases.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_dsa_political_complainant_pattern_through_august_2022","lenses":["4B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Women's participation in local government elections increases","publisher":"Bangladesh Sangbad Sangstha","publication_date":"2022-04-04","url":"https://www.bssnews.net/news/54147","claim":"Election Commission officials reported that women won 45 of 4,058 Union Parishad chairmanships in the June 2021-February 2022 election cycle, up from 26 of 4,087 in 2016; many winners ran as independents. This is concrete evidence of increased participation and successful competition, but women still held only about one percent of chair posts.","locator":"Lines 43-52: totals and comparison at 43-46; independent participation at 48-51; party-committee quota claim at 52","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-009","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_2021_2022_women_union_parishad_chair_wins","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.7"],"source_type":"official_election_statistics_reported_by_state_news","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Provides exact election-cycle denominators, comparator totals, and EC attribution, though it does not supply candidate totals or enforcement data for the party quota.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The election cycle ended in February 2022 and the results were reported in April 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A national participation outcome under Hasina's government; it is not evidence that Hasina personally selected, supported, or obstructed the candidates.","contrary_evidence":"Women increased their wins and some independents defeated party-backed rivals, but representation remained exceptionally low.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_2021_2022_women_union_parishad_chair_wins","lenses":["4B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Bangladesh: Freedom in the World 2023 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/bangladesh/freedom-world/2023","claim":"Freedom House found marginal improvement in 2022: Bangladesh's aggregate score rose from 39 to 40, independent candidates and defectors defeated AL candidates in the 2021-22 local-election cycle, and BNP held major protests. It nevertheless found continued AL consolidation, harassment, rally disruption, and a deeply impaired inherited electoral baseline. The comparison supports limited opening rather than a wholesale democratic recovery or further aggregate decline.","locator":"Score panel lines 59-74; overview and key developments lines 80-85; inherited election baseline lines 95-96; local-election and protest comparison lines 122-123","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-010","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_freedom_house_2022_change_from_2021","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.1","4B.10"],"source_type":"independent_expert_country_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"A transparent annual comparative assessment gives the current and prior score and identifies the target-year developments underlying the marginal change.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The 2023 edition explicitly assesses calendar year 2022 and compares it with the prior-year score.","ruler_attribution":"The assessment attributes consolidation and harassment principally to the ruling AL and authorities under Hasina; individual incidents should not automatically be treated as personally directed by her.","contrary_evidence":"The aggregate score increased one point; opposition rallies occurred and independent or defecting candidates defeated AL candidates.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_freedom_house_2022_change_from_2021","lenses":["4B.1","4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Democracy Index: Bangladesh moves two notches up","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2023-02-03","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/democracy-index-bangladesh-moves-two-notches-3238326","claim":"The EIU Democracy Index placed Bangladesh 73rd in 2022, up from 75th in 2021, but its score remained unchanged at 5.99 for a third consecutive year and it remained classified as a hybrid regime. The rank movement therefore does not demonstrate substantive domestic improvement and independently supports continuity from the inherited baseline.","locator":"Lines 198-210: ranks at 198-200; hybrid-regime classification at 203-206; unchanged 5.99 score at 208-210","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-011","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_eiu_democracy_index_2022_baseline_comparison","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.10"],"source_type":"independent_comparative_index_reported_by_press","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Exact score, classification, and year-on-year ranks are reported, but the underlying EIU report is proprietary and could not be directly inspected.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Explicit comparison of 2022 with 2021.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level comparative outcome during Hasina's rule; it does not isolate her personal conduct from institutions or broader conditions.","contrary_evidence":"Bangladesh's rank improved by two places, although its unchanged score shows that this could reflect changes elsewhere rather than domestic improvement.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_eiu_democracy_index_2022_baseline_comparison","lenses":["4B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"EC talks with parties: Supervisory govt sought for polls","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-08-01","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/politics/news/ec-talks-parties-supervisory-govt-sought-polls-3084476","claim":"During the EC's July-August 2022 party dialogue, the Awami League categorically maintained that the next national election would be held under the incumbent government and that restoration of a caretaker system was a closed chapter. Nearly half of registered parties instead favored an interim, caretaker, or all-party election-time government, while BNP and eight other parties boycotted the EC talks. This directly establishes the governing party's 2022 transfer position, but not a statement personally made by Hasina.","locator":"Opening through party-position section, especially paragraphs stating that 18 parties supported supervisory arrangements; AL position attributed to General Secretary Obaidul Quader; and the list of nine parties abstaining from EC dialogue","provisional_id":"WEB-4B-C1-012","canonical_fact_key":"bangladesh_awami_league_2022_caretaker_position","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["4B"],"methodology_ids":["4B.8"],"source_type":"independent_contemporaneous_political_reporting","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting distinguishes the positions of the governing party, opposition parties, and EC and quantifies participation in the dialogue.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct July-August 2022 governing-party position.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to Hasina's governing party and government policy; personal conduct by Hasina remains inferential because the quoted categorical rejection came from AL General Secretary Obaidul Quader.","contrary_evidence":"The AL publicly committed to elections under the constitutional framework and told the EC it supported free and credible elections, while the EC held consultations with 28 parties.","underlying_fact_key":"bangladesh_awami_league_2022_caretaker_position","lenses":["4B.8"]}
